{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-119647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At a geological site, a sample of a lunar rock is found with $84%$ of its original rubidium-87 content. Given that the half-life of rubidium-87 is $49.72$ billion years, approximately how old is the sample?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-119647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=49.72$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n49.72=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.84{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.84{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.84{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.84\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.84 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{49.72\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈12.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $12.5$ billion years old." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-119647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d504011541f7c2b38ad5d8ec1dc492940a2802379ba4b4c53189f7501d1f4786"} {"question" "q-119647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-119647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$12.5$ billion years"] ["text" "$25$ billion years"] ["text" "$3.125$ billion years"] ["text" "$50$ billion years"] ["text" "$6.25$ billion years"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-119647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$12.5$ billion years"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-120002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Samarium-147 has a half-life of $106.1$ billion years. A sample of samarium-147 has an initial mass of $300\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Rounded to the nearest integer, what is the mass of the sample after $80$ billion years?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-120002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=106.1$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n106.1=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{106.1}.\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=300\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=300{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/106.1}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nM(80)=300{e}^{-80\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/106.1}≈178\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that there will be $178\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ of the sample left." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-120002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d504011541f7c2b38ad5d8ec1dc492940a2802379ba4b4c53189f7501d1f4786"} {"question" "q-120002" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-120002" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "154\\,\\mu \\text{g}"] ["math" "168\\,\\mu \\text{g}"] ["math" "178\\,\\mu \\text{g}"] ["math" "185\\,\\mu \\text{g}"] ["math" "257\\,\\mu \\text{g}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-120002" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "178\\,\\mu \\text{g}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-353520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The half-life of cobalt-60 is $5.27$ years. How long does it take for a $8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of cobalt-60 to reduce to $0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{yr}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-353520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=5.27$ years, we have\n\n$$\n5.27=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\frac{0.5}{8.0} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{16}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡16 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡16 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5.27\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡16}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5.27\\cdot 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =21.08\\,\\text{yr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $4$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=4\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=4\\cdot 5.27=21.08\\,\\text{yr}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-353520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d504011541f7c2b38ad5d8ec1dc492940a2802379ba4b4c53189f7501d1f4786"} {"question" "q-353520" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-353520" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "21.08"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-353523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The half-life of fluorine-18 is $109.7$ minutes. How long does it take for a $6.4\\,\\text{ng}$ sample of fluorine-18 to reduce to $0.2\\,\\text{ng}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{min}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-353523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=109.7$ minutes, we have\n\n$$\n109.7=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=6.4\\,\\text{ng},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2 & =6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2 & =6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\frac{0.2}{6.4} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{32} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{32}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡32 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡32 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7} \\\\ t & =\\frac{109.7\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡32}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{109.7\\cdot 5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =548.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{0.2\\,\\text{ng}}{6.4\\,\\text{ng}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $5$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $6.4\\,\\text{ng}$ to $0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=5\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=5\\cdot 109.7=548.5\\,\\text{min}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-353523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d504011541f7c2b38ad5d8ec1dc492940a2802379ba4b4c53189f7501d1f4786"} {"question" "q-353523" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-353523" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "548.5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-355651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At a deep-sea sediment site, a sample is found with $30%$ of its original beryllium-10 content. Given that the half-life of beryllium-10 is $1.39$ million years, how old is the sample? Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\nThe sample is approximately\n{{field-1}}\nmillion years old." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-355651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=1.39$ million years, we have\n\n$$\n1.39=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{1.39}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.30{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.30{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.30{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39} \\\\ 0.30\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39} \\\\ 0.30 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30) & =ln\\,({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{1.39} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1.39\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $2.4$ million years old." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-355651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d504011541f7c2b38ad5d8ec1dc492940a2802379ba4b4c53189f7501d1f4786"} {"question" "q-355651" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"} {"question" "q-355651" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070579/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0d7b77391bccda1464e59b086c576cda1479407b8ec296f262966ffc723eff8"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2497 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
